  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThere has been no change to the expectations regarding the\nlarge-scale weather pattern over the next few days. A dome of \nhigh pressure (i.e surface high pressure underneath a stout \nupper ridge) strongly favors the persistence of dry weather and \nwell above normal temperature through the rest of the week. \nThere is potential for daytime highs and overnights lows end up \nvarying a degree or two from current forecasted values, but this\nwill not reduce the heat risk the region is set to experience. \nElevated dew points are likely to aid heat indices to regularly \ntouch 100 this week while limiting the degree of nocturnal \ncooling.\n\nThe main decision today was to expand the footprint of the Extreme  \nHeat Warning to most non-ridge zones in the CWA, while slightly \nexpanding the (now smaller) Heat Advisory. Not everyone is \nexpected to reach the heat index criteria of 105+ that is the \nbasis of such warnings. However, a few factors justify this \nupgrade. Many areas will experience 100+ heat index values over a \n4 day period, adding to the cumulative impact. Also, widespread \nwarm and muggy overnight conditions with minimum temperatures in \nthe lower to mid 70s will limit overnight recovery potential, \nespecially for those without access to reliable air conditioning. \nThis is reflected in NWS probabilistic Heat Risk values, with the \nmajority of the area in the Warning having a 60% or greater chance\nof reaching the Extreme (level 4 of 4) category on Thursday.\n\nThe highest elevation zones of eastern Preston and Tucker have\nnot been added to heat headline areas for now, in a nod to\nneighboring WFO collaboration as well as smaller areal/temporal\nextent of 100+ heat indices. However, probabilities here for at\nleast Major (level 3 of 4) Heat Risk remain quite high by \nThursday, and an eventual expansion of at least a Heat Advisory \nto these zones remains possible. Regardless of headline status, \nit remains best to start practicing heat risk mitigation efforts\nnow. More information on heat-related information can be found \nat weather.gov/safety/heat-tools.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThere remains good agreement among the various ensembles that\nbreakdown of the upper ridge appears likely from Friday into the\nweekend, as shortwaves coming over the ridge bring a reduction\nin 500mb heights. Of course, such summertime shifts from strong\nupper ridging to more of a WNW flow pattern often come with\nincreased severe weather risks. Regarding this period, all we \ncan say with any level of confidence at this time is that \nshower and thunderstorm chances will increase, and that \ntemperatures will begin to moderate. Finer details on timing,\nrainfall potential, and location/level of severe risks will have\nto wait until later this week, and even then, small mesoscale\ndetails could have big impacts on the overall outcomes. CIPS\nanalogs/long range machine-learning guidance continue to\nhighlight Friday and Saturday as the days to watch. \n\nGiven the potential higher impact of severe weather with planned\nholiday activities during the weekend, it is prudent for\ndecision makers to be alert of this risk and continue to track\nthe latest outlooks in the coming days.",
